  These aren't read-only problems, but they affect whether the "Read-only" view tells the truth:

  - [1mPossible flash of the wrong status.[0m [38;5;153monEnd[39m and [38;5;153monExit[39m are separate main-queue jobs (lines 135-136). If [38;5;153mTerminalStream
[39m    sends the end of output before the exit code, then for one frame [38;5;153mended == true[39m while [38;5;153mexitStatus[39m still holds its
    starting value of [38;5;153m0[39m. A dropped link shows "Finished" and then flips to "Lost the connection to this agent." Whether
    this happens depends on the order [38;5;153mTerminalStream[39m uses, which I can't see. A simple fix is to set [38;5;153mended[39m only when the
    exit code arrives.
  - [1mA failure that never produced output says "isn't running".[0m Line 28 returns before it looks at [38;5;153mexitStatus[39m. A wrong
    binary, an authentication failure or a refused connection (any nonzero exit with no output) all show "This agent
    isn't running right now." That tells the owner the agent is idle when the real problem is the connection.
  - The overlay on line 54 calls [38;5;153mstatus[39m without [38;5;153mexitStatus[39m. That is consistent today, because the no-output branch never
    reads it. It will quietly break if the no-output branch ever starts looking at the exit code.

